1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,898
Linux navigation.

2
00:00:00,898 --> 00:00:08,082
Use pwd, ls, and cd to move through a project without losing track of your location.

3
00:00:08,082 --> 00:00:12,122
The goal is clear: Report the current working directory.

4
00:00:12,972 --> 00:00:15,092
Your working directory is context.

5
00:00:15,092 --> 00:00:18,907
Relative paths are interpreted from the current working directory.

6
00:00:18,907 --> 00:00:22,297
Absolute paths begin at the filesystem root /.

7
00:00:22,297 --> 00:00:30,351
Many “missing file” errors are really context errors: the file exists, but not where the current command is looking.

8
00:00:30,351 --> 00:00:33,742
Use pwd before any command that writes output.

9
00:00:33,742 --> 00:00:37,980
Use ls to verify the next directory name before cd.

10
00:00:37,980 --> 00:00:45,610
The shortcuts dot and dot dot mean the current and parent directories, while tilde means your home directory.

11
00:00:46,460 --> 00:00:49,768
Now connect the idea to an observable check.

12
00:00:49,768 --> 00:00:51,837
First, print your current location.

13
00:00:51,837 --> 00:00:54,318
Next, create a course practice directory.

14
00:00:54,318 --> 00:00:58,041
Then, enter it and create data and output subfolders.

15
00:00:58,041 --> 00:01:02,177
After that, return to the parent directory using dot dot.

16
00:01:02,177 --> 00:01:05,486
The command shown on screen is an example.

17
00:01:05,486 --> 00:01:11,690
Adapt its path to your own working directory and verify the target before running it.

18
00:01:12,540 --> 00:01:14,418
Cannot find a file?

19
00:01:14,418 --> 00:01:19,115
Stop and verify context instead of repeatedly changing the command.

20
00:01:19,115 --> 00:01:23,341
Print the directory, list its contents, then search narrowly.

21
00:01:23,341 --> 00:01:28,507
A useful self-check is this: When is data/sub-001 a valid path?

22
00:01:28,507 --> 00:01:36,961
When a data directory exists inside the current working directory; otherwise use the correct relative or absolute path.

23
00:01:37,811 --> 00:01:44,157
To recap: First, use pwd then ls then cd as a deliberate loop.

24
00:01:44,157 --> 00:01:47,086
Next, create output directories before analysis.

25
00:01:47,086 --> 00:01:51,480
Then, most path errors are diagnosable with small checks.

26
00:01:51,480 --> 00:01:58,314
Before continuing, make sure you can explain this objective: Report the current working directory.
